# Node.js Runtime Best Practices

Directives for Node.js services, APIs, workers, and CLIs. Australian English spelling throughout.

---

## Runtime and Modules

- Target an active LTS (Node 22 or 24); declare it in `package.json` `engines` and `.nvmrc`/`.node-version`
- Prefer ESM (`"type": "module"`); import built-ins with the `node:` prefix
- Use built-ins before dependencies: `fetch`, `AbortSignal.timeout()`, `node:test`, `--watch`, `--env-file`, `util.parseArgs`
- TypeScript: Node 22.18+/23.6+ strips erasable types natively (no flag). Use it for scripts and dev; enable `erasableSyntaxOnly` in `tsconfig`. Still run `tsc --noEmit` for type checking, and compile (`tsc`/bundler) for production if you need non-erasable syntax

---

## Configuration

- Read `process.env` in one config module only; validate with Zod at startup and fail fast
- Export a single typed, frozen `config` object
- Never default-fill secrets; explicit defaults only for non-sensitive optional values

---

## Async Errors

- Every promise is awaited, returned, or explicitly supervised — no floating promises (`@typescript-eslint/no-floating-promises`)
- Express 5 forwards rejected handler promises to error middleware; Express 4 needs a wrapper (see `api-design-typescript.mdc`)
- Register `process.on('unhandledRejection')` / `uncaughtException` to log and exit — do not try to keep running
- Set timeouts on all outbound I/O (`AbortSignal.timeout(ms)`); retry only idempotent operations, with backoff and jitter
- Map unexpected errors to safe responses; never leak stack traces to clients

---

## Process Lifecycle

- Handle `SIGTERM` and `SIGINT`: stop accepting new work → drain in-flight requests/messages → close DB pools, consumers, and the HTTP server → exit
- Bound the shutdown with a timeout, then force exit with non-zero code
- Exit non-zero on unrecoverable startup failures
- Do not block the event loop: offload CPU-heavy work to `worker_threads` or a job queue; use streams with `pipeline()` for large payloads

---

## Observability

- Structured JSON logs (Pino) with consistent fields; redact secrets
- Propagate a request/correlation ID using `AsyncLocalStorage`
- Expose `/health` (liveness) and `/ready` (dependencies) for services
- Instrument with OpenTelemetry (`@opentelemetry/sdk-node`, loaded before app code via `--import`)

---

## Checklist

- [ ] LTS Node version pinned in `engines`
- [ ] Config validated once at startup; no stray `process.env` reads
- [ ] No floating promises; outbound calls have timeouts
- [ ] Graceful shutdown on SIGTERM/SIGINT with bounded timeout
- [ ] Structured logs with correlation IDs; health endpoints present
